Fresh roses can vary in opening stage, stem strength, and visual symmetry. For importers and distributors, those differences quickly become claims, waste, and inconsistent customer experiences. Quality control is the bridge between harvesting and export readiness.

## The challenge of non-standard flowers

When a rose does not meet export standards, common issues appear:

- Incorrect opening stage
- Inconsistent stem length
- Mechanical damage on petals
- Hidden phytosanitary problems
- Stem curvature
- Early dehydration

## How Lladeal improves post-harvest quality

Lladeal applies a stricter technical review process so each bunch reaches a uniform commercial standard before shipment.

## Advanced quality control techniques

### Opening stage management

Lladeal works with clear stage definitions by variety, helping the field team cut at the correct moment so boxes open more evenly at destination.

### Stem and length standardization

Each stem is checked not only for length, but also for caliber and structural resistance so the flower can support the bud during transport.

### Defect detection and discard process

Protection petals and bud shape are reviewed carefully to identify deformities, bruises, or hidden damage before export.

### Hydration and sanitation control

Hydration formulas and handling temperatures are adjusted according to variety and transit time to protect vase life.

### Foliage cleaning and preparation

Proper lower-stem cleaning improves airflow inside the box and reduces contamination risks during long-distance shipping.

## Why this matters for importers

Better quality control means lower claims, longer vase life, and more consistent product presentation in every shipment.

## Conclusion

For premium export roses, quality control is not optional. It is the process that protects value from the farm to the final customer. By applying Lladeal's strict standards, importers receive a cleaner, more predictable, and more reliable flower.
